Nothing to guess here: run the example and read the result below. The Learn step explains why it behaves this way. Every line uses one idea from Weeks 8 to 14.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

void bump(int x) { x += 1; }
void bump_ref(int& x) { x += 1; }

int main() {
    int a = 1;
    bump(a);
    bump_ref(a);
    std::cout << a << '\n';                 // line 1

    std::vector<int> v{1, 2, 3};
    std::vector<int> w = v;                 // copy
    w.push_back(4);
    std::cout << v.size() << ' ' << w.size() << '\n';   // line 2

    auto p = std::make_unique<std::string>("hi");
    auto q = std::move(p);
    std::cout << (p ? "p owns" : "p empty") << ' ' << *q << '\n';  // line 3

    auto s1 = std::make_shared<int>(7);
    auto s2 = s1;
    std::cout << s1.use_count() << '\n';    // line 4
}
```

**It prints** `2`, `3 4`, `p empty hi`, `2`. If a line isn't clear yet, the Learn step reviews that topic.

Eight weeks of C++ boil down to a small number of habits. This lesson puts them side by side so you can see that they are one way of thinking, not eight topics.

**1. Values by default, references on purpose.** A C++ variable *is* its object, not a pointer to it. `w = v` copies the whole vector. That is a feature: values are easy to reason about. You opt out of copying deliberately: `const T&` for read-only parameters of non-trivial types, `T&` when the function must change the caller's object, and plain `T` for small things (`int`, `double`, `std::string_view`) or when you want your own copy anyway (then sort or modify it freely).

**2. Ownership is explicit.** Every heap resource has exactly one owner that frees it: a container, a `std::unique_ptr`, or (rarely) a group of `std::shared_ptr`s. You never write `delete`. This is RAII: the destructor releases the resource, and destructors run on every exit path, including exceptions. A raw pointer or reference in modern code means "I am looking at this, I do not own it".

**3. The standard library does the loops.** `std::sort`, `std::find_if`, `std::count_if`, `std::accumulate`, `std::max_element` say what you mean and are hard to get wrong at the edges. `std::map` keeps keys sorted (tree, O(log n)); `std::unordered_map` hashes (O(1) average, no order).

**4. Classes protect an invariant.** A class earns its existence when some rule must always hold, such as "this buffer never has more than N items". Data is private, constructors establish the rule, member functions keep it, and functions that do not change state are marked `const`.

**5. Templates write the type for you.** `template <typename T>` is a recipe the compiler instantiates per type. Templates live entirely in headers, because the compiler needs the full definition at each use.

**Five bugs you should now recognise on sight:**

- Returning a reference to a local: `const std::string& name() { std::string s = "x"; return s; }`. The local dies at `}`.
- Using an iterator or reference into a `std::vector` after `push_back`. A push can reallocate and move every element.
- A `std::string_view` built from a temporary: `std::string_view sv = std::string("abc") + "d";` dangles at the semicolon.
- Deleting a derived object through a base pointer whose destructor is not `virtual`. Only the base part is destroyed.
- `for (int i = 0; i < v.size() - 1; ++i)` with an empty `v`: `v.size() - 1` is unsigned and wraps to a huge number. Compile with `-Wall -Wextra` and the compiler warns about the comparison.

**Worked example.** A small statistics helper that uses all five habits: values and const references, a class with an invariant, algorithms instead of loops, a template, and no manual memory management.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <cassert>
#include <iostream>
#include <numeric>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <vector>

// A fixed-capacity window of the most recent readings (invariant: size() <= capacity).
template <typename T>
class Window {
public:
    explicit Window(std::size_t capacity) : cap_(capacity) {
        if (cap_ == 0) throw std::invalid_argument("capacity must be > 0");
    }
    void push(T x) {
        if (data_.size() == cap_) data_.erase(data_.begin());  // drop the oldest
        data_.push_back(x);
    }
    std::size_t size() const { return data_.size(); }
    double mean() const {
        if (data_.empty()) throw std::logic_error("empty window");
        return std::accumulate(data_.begin(), data_.end(), 0.0) / data_.size();
    }
    T max() const { return *std::max_element(data_.begin(), data_.end()); }
private:
    std::size_t cap_;
    std::vector<T> data_;
};

// Read-only access: const reference. Counting: an algorithm, not a loop.
long count_above(const std::vector<int>& xs, int limit) {
    return std::count_if(xs.begin(), xs.end(), [limit](int x) { return x > limit; });
}

int main() {
    Window<int> w(3);
    for (int x : {10, 20, 30, 40}) w.push(x);   // 10 falls out
    assert(w.size() == 3);
    assert(w.mean() == 30.0);
    assert(w.max() == 40);
    assert(count_above({5, 50, 500}, 10) == 2);
    std::cout << "mean of last 3: " << w.mean() << '\n';   // 30
}
```

Read it once more and name the habit behind each line. Note `erase(begin())` is O(n): fine for a window of 10, wrong for a window of 100,000. The practice asks you for a ring buffer that avoids it, which is exactly the structure you will need when the Arduino streams readings to your dashboard later this week.

