tasks.solution:
Taking `v` by value gives the function its own copy, so sorting it does not reorder the caller's data. That is clearer than taking a const reference and copying inside.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <vector>

// By value: we sort our own copy and leave the caller's vector alone.
double median(std::vector<double> v) {
    if (v.empty()) throw std::invalid_argument("empty");
    std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());
    std::size_t n = v.size();
    if (n % 2 == 1) return v[n / 2];
    return (v[n / 2 - 1] + v[n / 2]) / 2.0;
}

int main() { return median({1.0, 2.0, 3.0}) == 2.0 ? 0 : 1; }
```

