steps.body:
Until now the serial monitor has been a debugging window. From this week it is a **data link**: the Arduino measures, the laptop computes and plots. ROS 2's micro-ROS, which your students meet in the autumn, rests on exactly this link.

**UART basics.** The ATmega328P sends bytes one bit at a time on its TX pin. Each byte is framed as **8N1**: one start bit, 8 data bits, no parity, one stop bit, so **10 bits per byte**. At 115200 baud (bits per second) that is 11,520 bytes/s; at 9600 baud, 960 bytes/s. Both ends must agree on the baud rate or the receiver decodes garbage.

**The USB bridge.** On an Uno, a second chip (an ATmega16U2, or a CH340 on many clones) turns that UART into a USB serial device. On your Mac it appears as `/dev/cu.usbmodem…` (genuine Uno) or `/dev/cu.usbserial-…` / `/dev/cu.wchusbserial…` (CH340 clones). List ports with `python3 -m serial.tools.list_ports`. Use the `cu.` device, not `tty.`.

**Two traps.** Opening the port toggles the DTR line, which **resets the Arduino**: the bootloader runs for about 1–2 s, then your sketch restarts. And only **one program** can hold the port: close the Arduino IDE's Serial Monitor before running Python.

**Arduino side.** Print one line per sample, separated by commas, timed with `millis()` (week 12), not `delay()`:

```arduino
const unsigned long PERIOD_MS = 50;   // 20 samples per second
unsigned long last = 0;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
}

void loop() {
  unsigned long now = millis();
  if (now - last >= PERIOD_MS) {
    last += PERIOD_MS;
    Serial.print(now);
    Serial.print(',');
    Serial.println(analogRead(A0));   // println ends the line with \r\n
  }
}
```

**Python side** (`pip install pyserial`; the import name is `serial`):

```python-snippet
import time
import serial

PORT = "/dev/cu.usbmodem14101"        # yours will differ: list_ports
with serial.Serial(PORT, 115200, timeout=1) as ser:
    time.sleep(2)                     # the board resets on open
    ser.reset_input_buffer()          # drop anything sent during boot
    ser.readline()                    # the first line may be partial
    for _ in range(20):
        raw = ser.readline()          # bytes up to b"\n", or b"" on timeout
        t_ms, a0 = raw.decode("ascii", errors="replace").strip().split(",")
        print(int(t_ms), int(a0))
```

Worked number: a line like `12345,678\r\n` is 11 bytes. At 115200 baud the link can carry 11520 / 11 ≈ 1047 such lines per second; at 9600 baud only 960 / 11 ≈ 87. At 20 lines per second both are fine, but at 9600 baud a sketch that prints every loop will fill the 64-byte transmit buffer and `Serial.print` starts to **block**, slowing your whole loop.

