steps.body:
A serial link is just a stream of bytes. The receiver must work out where each message starts and ends, and whether it arrived intact. That is called **framing**, and there are two common designs.

**Text lines (what you used yesterday).** Fields separated by commas, messages ended by `\n`. The newline *is* the frame. Pros: readable in the Serial Monitor, trivial to debug, works with any terminal. Cons: variable length, numbers cost one byte per digit, and corruption is detected only if it breaks the format (a flipped bit in `512` giving `513` passes unnoticed).

**Binary packets.** A fixed layout like this one, which you will use in the lab:

| bytes | content |
|---|---|
| 0–1 | header `0xAA 0x55` (marks a start) |
| 2 | payload length (8) |
| 3–10 | payload: `uint32 t_ms`, `uint16 a0`, `uint16 a1`, little-endian |
| 11 | checksum: XOR of the 8 payload bytes |

12 bytes per sample instead of about 17 for `123456,1023,512\r\n`, every value exact, and a checksum to catch most corruption. Cons: unreadable by eye, and both sides must agree on byte order and sizes. The Uno is little-endian with 16-bit `int` and 32-bit `unsigned long`; Python's `struct` format `"<IHH"` describes exactly that payload.

**Recovering from errors.** The parser is a small state machine: search for `0xAA 0x55`, read the length, wait until the whole packet is there, check the checksum; on a bad checksum, drop **one** byte and search again (the real header may be inside the bad packet).

```arduino
void sendPacket(uint32_t t, uint16_t a0, uint16_t a1) {
  uint8_t p[8];
  memcpy(p, &t, 4); memcpy(p + 4, &a0, 2); memcpy(p + 6, &a1, 2);
  uint8_t cs = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++) cs ^= p[i];
  Serial.write(0xAA); Serial.write(0x55); Serial.write(8);
  Serial.write(p, 8);
  Serial.write(cs);
}
```

Rule of thumb you will give students: **start with text lines**. Move to binary only when you measure that the link is too slow or you need guaranteed integrity.

This week's C++ project is `logstat`: a small tool that reads a CSV log like the one your Arduino will stream this week (`t_ms,pot,light`) and prints count, min, max and mean for one column. It is small on purpose. The point is the shape every serious command-line program has.

**The shape.** Split the program into three layers:

1. **Parse arguments** into a plain `struct Options`. No I/O, no exiting: it either returns options or throws.
2. **Compute** from an `std::istream&` into a `struct Stats`. No `argv`, no files: pure logic you can test with a string.
3. **`main`** (or a `run` function `main` calls) opens the file, catches errors, prints, and returns an exit code.

Layers 1 and 2 are where the bugs live, and both are testable without touching the disk. `main` becomes so thin that there is little left to test by hand.

**argc and argv.** `int main(int argc, char* argv[])` receives the words of the command line. `argv[0]` is the program name, `argv[1]` to `argv[argc-1]` are the arguments, and `argv[argc]` is a null pointer. Copy them into a `std::vector<std::string>` right away (`std::vector<std::string> args(argv + 1, argv + argc);`) and never touch `char*` again.

**Converting text to numbers.** `std::stoi` and `std::stod` throw `std::invalid_argument` when nothing parses and `std::out_of_range` on overflow, but they silently accept trailing junk (`"12abc"` gives 12). For strict parsing, check the `pos` out-parameter (`std::stod(s, &pos)` then require `pos == s.size()`), or use `std::from_chars` from `<charconv>`.

**Exit codes.** Return 0 on success. By common convention, 2 means "you called me wrong" (usage error) and 1 means "something went wrong while working". Scripts and CI rely on this. Errors go to `std::cerr`, results to `std::cout`, so `logstat x.csv > out.txt` still shows errors on screen.

**Bad lines are data, not crashes.** A serial log always contains a half line from when the port opened. The compute layer should skip unparseable lines and count them, and the output should report how many were skipped.

**Worked example: the argument layer.**

```cpp
#include <cassert>
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

struct Options {
    int column = 1;            // 0-based column to summarise
    bool skip_header = false;
    std::string path;          // empty means "read standard input"
};

int to_int_strict(const std::string& s) {
    std::size_t pos = 0;
    int v = std::stoi(s, &pos);            // throws if nothing parses
    if (pos != s.size()) throw std::invalid_argument("not an integer: " + s);
    return v;
}

Options parse_args(const std::vector<std::string>& args) {
    Options o;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < args.size(); ++i) {
        const std::string& a = args[i];
        if (a == "--column") {
            if (i + 1 >= args.size()) throw std::invalid_argument("--column needs a value");
            o.column = to_int_strict(args[++i]);
            if (o.column < 0) throw std::invalid_argument("--column must be >= 0");
        } else if (a == "--skip-header") {
            o.skip_header = true;
        } else if (!a.empty() && a[0] == '-') {
            throw std::invalid_argument("unknown option " + a);
        } else if (o.path.empty()) {
            o.path = a;
        } else {
            throw std::invalid_argument("only one file allowed");
        }
    }
    return o;
}

int main() {
    Options o = parse_args({"--column", "2", "--skip-header", "sensor.csv"});
    assert(o.column == 2 && o.skip_header && o.path == "sensor.csv");
    bool threw = false;
    try { parse_args({"--column", "2x"}); } catch (const std::invalid_argument&) { threw = true; }
    assert(threw);
    std::cout << "column " << o.column << " of " << o.path << '\n';
}
```

Notice that `parse_args` takes a vector, not `argc/argv`. The real `main` builds that vector in one line, and the tests build it from a literal. The same trick makes the compute layer take `std::istream&`: in tests you hand it `std::istringstream("1,2\n3,4\n")`, in the program an `std::ifstream`.

