steps.body:
So far data flowed one way. Now Python sends commands: change the LED brightness, change the sample rate, ask "are you alive?". The robot version of this, later, is a laptop sending speed commands to a motor board.

**The protocol.** One command per line, a word and optional number, one reply per command:

| Python sends | Arduino replies |
|---|---|
| `PING` | `PONG` |
| `LED 0..255` | `OK LED 128` |
| `RATE 10..1000` (ms) | `OK RATE 50` |
| anything else | `ERR unknown` / `ERR range` |

Replies start with `OK`, `ERR` or `PONG` so Python can tell them apart from data lines (which start with a digit). Every command gets exactly one reply, so the sender always knows whether it worked.

**Non-blocking reading on the Arduino.** `Serial.readStringUntil('\n')` waits (up to a 1-second timeout) for the rest of the line, freezing your sampling. Instead, collect characters into a buffer as they arrive and act only when `\n` comes:

```arduino
char buf[24];
byte len = 0;
int ledLevel = 0;
unsigned long period = 50, last = 0;

void handle(char* line) {
  if (strcmp(line, "PING") == 0) { Serial.println("PONG"); return; }
  char* arg = strchr(line, ' ');
  if (!arg) { Serial.println("ERR unknown"); return; }
  *arg++ = '\0';
  long v = atol(arg);
  if (strcmp(line, "LED") == 0) {
    if (v < 0 || v > 255) { Serial.println("ERR range"); return; }
    ledLevel = v; analogWrite(9, ledLevel);
    Serial.print("OK LED "); Serial.println(ledLevel);
  } else if (strcmp(line, "RATE") == 0) {
    if (v < 10 || v > 1000) { Serial.println("ERR range"); return; }
    period = v;
    Serial.print("OK RATE "); Serial.println(period);
  } else {
    Serial.println("ERR unknown");
  }
}

void setup() { Serial.begin(115200); pinMode(9, OUTPUT); }

void loop() {
  while (Serial.available()) {
    char c = Serial.read();
    if (c == '\r') continue;
    if (c == '\n') { buf[len] = '\0'; handle(buf); len = 0; }
    else if (len < sizeof(buf) - 1) buf[len++] = c;   // overlong lines are truncated
  }
  unsigned long now = millis();
  if (now - last >= period) {
    last = now;
    Serial.print(now); Serial.print(','); Serial.println(analogRead(A0));
  }
}
```

**Python side.** Send bytes ending in `\n` and wait for the reply, skipping data lines, with a deadline:

```python-snippet
def command(ser, text, timeout=0.5):
    ser.write((text + "\n").encode("ascii"))
    deadline = time.monotonic() + timeout
    while time.monotonic() < deadline:
        line = ser.readline().decode("ascii", "replace").strip()
        if line.startswith(("OK", "ERR", "PONG")):
            return line
    raise TimeoutError(text)
```

If the reader thread from yesterday owns the port, it must route reply lines to a second queue instead of the plot queue; you will do that in the build block.

The Arduino's `handle` logic is small enough to mirror in Python, which lets you test the protocol and the Python `command` function together before flashing anything:

```python
class FakeBoard:
    # Mirrors the sketch's command handling, including its replies.
    def __init__(self):
        self.led = 0
        self.period = 50
        self.out = []

    def write(self, data):
        for line in data.decode("ascii").splitlines():
            self.out.append(self.handle(line.strip()))

    def handle(self, line):
        if line == "PING":
            return "PONG"
        word, _, arg = line.partition(" ")
        if not arg or not arg.lstrip("-").isdigit():
            return "ERR unknown"
        v = int(arg)
        if word == "LED":
            if not 0 <= v <= 255:
                return "ERR range"
            self.led = v
            return f"OK LED {v}"
        if word == "RATE":
            if not 10 <= v <= 1000:
                return "ERR range"
            self.period = v
            return f"OK RATE {v}"
        return "ERR unknown"

b = FakeBoard()
for cmd in ["PING", "LED 128", "LED 300", "RATE 20", "SPIN 3", "LED"]:
    b.write((cmd + "\n").encode())
print(b.out)
assert b.out == ["PONG", "OK LED 128", "ERR range", "OK RATE 20", "ERR unknown", "ERR unknown"]
assert b.led == 128 and b.period == 20
```

Then in Wokwi: Uno, pot on A0, LED with 220 Ω on pin 9. Type `LED 30`, `LED 255`, `RATE 500` into the Serial Monitor (set it to send a newline) and confirm the LED and the data rate change while samples keep arriving.

