With `-fsanitize=address`, AddressSanitizer stops the program at the read with a `heap-use-after-free` report, pointing at both the `push_back` that freed the buffer and the line that read it. Learn explains how to use it.

Yesterday `logstat` became a set of pure functions plus a thin `main`. Today it gets the two things that separate a script from a tool: automatic tests anyone can run with one command, and a public home.

**Project layout.**

```bash
logstat/
  CMakeLists.txt
  README.md
  .gitignore          # build/
  src/logstat.hpp     # Options, Stats, declarations
  src/logstat.cpp     # parse_args, field_as_double, summarize, run
  src/main.cpp        # the four-line main
  tests/test_logstat.cpp
```

The logic goes in a library target; `main.cpp` and the test file both link against it. Nothing is compiled twice and the tests exercise exactly the code the program runs.

```cmake
cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.20)
project(logstat CXX)
set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 20)
set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD_REQUIRED ON)

add_library(logstat_core src/logstat.cpp)
target_include_directories(logstat_core PUBLIC src)
target_compile_options(logstat_core PRIVATE -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic)

add_executable(logstat src/main.cpp)
target_link_libraries(logstat PRIVATE logstat_core)

enable_testing()
add_executable(test_logstat tests/test_logstat.cpp)
target_link_libraries(test_logstat PRIVATE logstat_core)
add_test(NAME unit COMMAND test_logstat)
```

Then `cmake -S . -B build && cmake --build build && ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure`. `ctest` runs each registered test and treats a non-zero exit as failure, and a failed `assert` aborts with a non-zero status. That is all a test framework fundamentally is; GoogleTest and Catch2 add nicer reporting, which you can adopt later.

**Table-driven tests.** Instead of twenty near-identical asserts, put cases in an array and loop. Adding a case becomes one line, and a failure message can say which case broke:

```cpp
#include <cassert>
#include <cmath>
#include <iostream>
#include <optional>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

std::optional<double> parse_volts(const std::string& s) {
    try {
        std::size_t pos = 0;
        double v = std::stod(s, &pos);
        if (pos != s.size()) return std::nullopt;
        return v;
    } catch (...) { return std::nullopt; }
}

struct Case { std::string input; std::optional<double> expected; };

int main() {
    std::vector<Case> cases = {
        {"3.3", 3.3}, {"0", 0.0}, {"-1.5", -1.5}, {"", std::nullopt},
        {"3.3V", std::nullopt}, {"abc", std::nullopt},
    };
    int failures = 0;
    for (const auto& c : cases) {
        auto got = parse_volts(c.input);
        bool ok = got.has_value() == c.expected.has_value() &&
                  (!got || std::fabs(*got - *c.expected) < 1e-9);
        if (!ok) { ++failures; std::cerr << "FAIL: \"" << c.input << "\"\n"; }
    }
    std::cout << cases.size() - failures << "/" << cases.size() << " passed\n";
    return failures == 0 ? 0 : 1;
}
```

Never compare doubles with `==` unless the value is exactly representable and computed exactly (like `mean` of 4 and 8). Use a tolerance.

**Sanitizers.** Add a debug configuration that compiles with `-fsanitize=address,undefined -g`. AddressSanitizer catches use-after-free, buffer overflows and leaks; UBSan catches signed overflow, bad shifts and misaligned access. They slow the program 2 to 3 times, so they are for tests, not release builds. With CMake: `cmake -S . -B build-asan -DCMAKE_CXX_FLAGS="-fsanitize=address,undefined -g"`. Apple clang supports both on macOS (LeakSanitizer is limited there; it works fully on Linux).

**Publishing.** A README for a CLI answers, in order: what it does in one sentence, an example command and its output, how to build, how to run the tests, the input format, and the exit codes. Then commit, tag and push:

```bash
git tag -a v0.1.0 -m "logstat 0.1.0: column stats for CSV logs"
git push origin main --tags
```

On GitHub, turn the tag into a release with two lines of notes. Semantic versioning: 0.x means "the interface may still change"; bump the patch number for fixes, the minor number for new options.

